<p class="sst-lead">Center the curve by subtracting its arc-length mean,</p>
<p>\[ \mathcal{T}[\bm{c}](s)=\bm{c}(s)-\int_{\mathcal{I}}\bm{c}(u)\,d\mu(u). \]</p>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>Then form the <strong>composite linear operator</strong> (CLO) &mdash; every pairwise inner product of
  centered landmarks,</p>
  <p>\[ k_{\mathcal{T}}[\bm{c}](s,u)=\mathcal{T}[\bm{c}](s)\cdot\mathcal{T}[\bm{c}](u). \]</p>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p>Because it is built from dot products of <em>centered</em> coordinates, the CLO encodes all pairwise
  <strong>angles and relative lengths</strong> &mdash; the rigid-invariant content of the shape &mdash; while
  discarding absolute position and orientation.</p>
</div>

<div class="sst-frag">
  <p class="sst-claim">Claim: \(k_{\mathcal{T}}\) is invariant to <strong>p</strong>ermutation,
  <strong>r</strong>otation, <strong>r</strong>eflection, and <strong>t</strong>ranslation &mdash;
  it is <strong>PRRTI</strong>. Let's see it.</p>
</div>
